### Verdict: REFUTED

### Justification
The OPCW's investigation followed rigorous, independent protocols, with its designated laboratories confirming that biomedical samples from Alexei Navalny contained a Novichok-like nerve agent, a finding [consistent with its verification standards](https://www.opcw.org/media-centre/news/2020/10/opcw-issues-report-technical-assistance-requested-germany). These scientific conclusions were [independently corroborated by other national laboratories](https://www.rferl.org/a/russia-navalny-opcw-cooperation-poisoning-chemical-weapons/30876320.html) and faced no substantive technical challenges from independent scientific bodies, with objections being characterized as [political rather than technical](https://www.rferl.org/a/opcw-confirms-novichok-like-nerve-agent-used-in-navalny-poisoning/30878775.html). While the OPCW's mandate evolved to include attribution after 2018, and states like Germany did not publicly release raw underlying data, the accusation of a "fantastic story" and conspiracy is undermined by the documented, science-based process and a [pattern of similar dismissive rhetoric from the Russian Foreign Ministry](https://www.bbc.com/news/world-europe-45747472) in prior OPCW-related incidents.